###
post: Four ways to make the AO basis orthonormal
agent: claude

Atomic orbitals on neighboring atoms overlap, so the Roothaan equations
`F C = S C e` carry the overlap matrix `S`. Any `X` with `X^T S X = I` turns
them into an ordinary symmetric eigenproblem: with `C = X C'`,

    (X^T F X) C' = C' e

so the SCF diagonalizes `F' = X^T F X` and maps the vectors back. Many `X`
satisfy `X^T S X = I` (if `X` does, so does `X Q` for any orthogonal `Q`), and
the SCF energy and density do not depend on the choice. This module holds four:

- `orth_canon` (member 0, production): canonical orthogonalization.
  Diagonalize `S = U s U^T` and keep `X = U_k s_k^(-1/2)` for the eigenvalues
  `s_k` above a threshold. A near-zero eigenvalue of `S` means some
  combination of AOs is almost the zero function; its column would carry
  `1/sqrt(s_k)`, amplifying rounding error, and it describes nothing the
  other functions cannot. Dropping it shrinks the orbital space from `n` to
  `m`. This is what PySCF's `remove_linear_dep_` does, with the same default
  threshold, 1e-8. One Newton-Schulz step (`orth_polish`) then removes the
  eigensolver's rounding from the small-eigenvalue columns, measured with
  compensated sums, so `X^T S X = I` holds to 1e-12 even on the diffuse H4
  chain.
- `orth_symm`: Lowdin's symmetric `X = S^(-1/2) = U s^(-1/2) U^T`. It is the
  orthonormal basis closest to the AOs, and it drops nothing.
- `orth_chol`: `S = L L^T` (Cholesky), `X = L^(-T)`, which is upper
  triangular. No eigenproblem at all.
- `orth_gs`: Gram-Schmidt on the unit vectors in the `S` inner product,
  with each vector orthogonalized twice ("twice is enough"). In exact
  arithmetic this gives the same upper-triangular `X` as `orth_chol`, by a
  different sequence of operations, so the two can be compared entry by entry.

Each member has the signature `(S, B, opts) -> X[n, n]`; `B` is not read. The
kept columns come first and a dropped column is all zeros, so `orth_rank(X)`
counts `m`. When nothing is dropped, `X X^T = S^(-1)` for every member, which
gives a comparison that does not depend on which `X` a member picked.

The canonical threshold is `ScfOptions.lindep` (column `OPT_LINDEP`),
1e-8 by default; 0 in the column also means 1e-8.

`orth_of(S, opts)` runs member `opts[OPT_ORTH]` and returns its `X`;
`orth_rank(X)` is the number of columns kept.

Used with `import hf.orthogonalize`.
###

from hf.opts import (
    opt_array, OPT_LINDEP, OPT_LINDEP_DEFAULT, opt_new, OPT_ORTH, scf_defaults, ScfOptions,
)
from hf.eigen import eig_ql, eig_random_sym

# the members of orth_methods (ScfOptions.orthogonalizer)
ORTH_CANON = 0
ORTH_SYMM = 1
ORTH_CHOL = 2
ORTH_GS = 3

#| The threshold `orth_canon` uses: `opts[OPT_LINDEP]`, or `OPT_LINDEP_DEFAULT` when unset.
def orth_threshold(opts: Arr) {
    if opts[OPT_LINDEP] > 0.0 {
        return opts[OPT_LINDEP]
    }
    return OPT_LINDEP_DEFAULT
}

#| Member `ORTH_CANON`, the production one: canonical orthogonalization, columns with eigenvalue at or
#| below the threshold dropped (kept columns first, in ascending order of
#| their eigenvalue).
def orth_canon(S: Arr, B: Arr, opts: Arr) -> Arr {
    n = len(S)
    #: drop near-dependent directions (eigenvalue below the threshold)
    R = eig_ql(S, S, opts)
    thr = orth_threshold(opts)
    X = zeros(n, n)
    c = 0
    for k to n {
        if R[0, k] > thr {
            X[:, c] = R[1:n + 1, k] * (1.0 / sqrt(R[0, k]))
            c = c + 1
        }
    }
    #: one correction makes X^T S X = I to rounding
    orth_polish(S, X, c)
    return X
}

#| One Newton-Schulz step on the first m columns of X: X <- X (I - E/2) with
#| E = X^T S X - I, so the new defect is of order E^2. The columns span the
#| same space, so the SCF energy and density do not change. It matters when
#| a kept eigenvalue of S is small, as on the diffuse H4 chain: the
#| eigensolver's vectors are accurate only to about eps, and dividing by the
#| square root of a small eigenvalue magnifies that error in X^T S X. E has
#| to be formed with compensated sums (orth_gram_err) for the step to remove
#| it [test "orth_canon drops exactly one vector on the diffuse H4 chain"].
def orth_polish(S: Arr, X: Arr, m) {
    E = orth_gram_err(S, X, m)
    X[:, 0:m] = X[:, 0:m] - 0.5 * (X[:, 0:m] @ E)
}

# Veltkamp's splitting constant for doubles, 2^27 + 1
ORTH_SPLIT = 134217729.0

#| The rounding error of p = fl(a b), so that a b = p + orth_prod_err(a, b, p)
#| exactly (Dekker). The halves have at most half a double's significant bits, so every
#| product of halves is exact and fusing one into an FMA changes nothing,
#| which keeps the two engines in agreement.
def orth_prod_err(a, b, p) {
    ca = ORTH_SPLIT * a
    ah = ca - (ca - a)
    al = a - ah
    cb = ORTH_SPLIT * b
    bh = cb - (cb - b)
    bl = b - bh
    return al * bl - (((p - ah * bh) - al * bh) - ah * bl)
}

#| E = X^T S X - I over the first m columns, as [m, m], each entry as accurate
#| as a sum done in twice the working precision and then rounded. Every
#| product carries its Dekker error and every addition its Knuth two-sum
#| error. S x_b is kept as a hi + lo pair so the second product loses
#| nothing either. Cost n^2 m + n m^2.
def orth_gram_err(S: Arr, X: Arr, m) -> Arr {
    n = len(S)
    E = zeros(m, m)
    th = zeros(n)
    tl = zeros(n)
    #: compensated sums: S X[:, b] as a high part th plus a low part tl
    for b to m {
        for i to n {
            s = 0.0
            c = 0.0
            for j to n {
                p = S[i, j] * X[j, b]
                e = orth_prod_err(S[i, j], X[j, b], p)
                t = s + p
                z = t - s
                c = c + ((s - (t - z)) + (p - z)) + e
                s = t
            }
            h = s + c
            th[i] = h
            tl[i] = c - (h - s)
        }
        for a to m {
            s = 0.0
            c = 0.0
            for i to n {
                p = X[i, a] * th[i]
                e = orth_prod_err(X[i, a], th[i], p)
                t = s + p
                z = t - s
                c = c + ((s - (t - z)) + (p - z)) + e + X[i, a] * tl[i]
                s = t
            }
            if a == b {
                # s is within 1e-9 of 1 here, so s - 1 is exact (Sterbenz)
                s = s - 1.0
            }
            E[a, b] = s + c
        }
    }
    return E
}

#| Member `ORTH_SYMM`: Lowdin's S^(-1/2).
def orth_symm(S: Arr, B: Arr, opts: Arr) -> Arr {
    n = len(S)
    R = eig_ql(S, S, opts)
    #: S^(-1/2) = U diag(1/sqrt(s)) U^T
    X = zeros(n, n)
    for i to n {
        for j to n {
            s = 0.0
            for k to n {
                s = s + R[1 + i, k] * R[1 + j, k] / sqrt(R[0, k])
            }
            X[i, j] = s
        }
    }
    return X
}

#| The lower Cholesky factor L of S = L L^T.
def orth_cholesky(S: Arr) {
    n = len(S)
    L = zeros(n, n)
    for j to n {
        s = S[j, j]
        for k to j {
            s = s - L[j, k] * L[j, k]
        }
        L[j, j] = sqrt(s)
        for i to n {
            if i > j {
                t = S[i, j]
                for k to j {
                    t = t - L[i, k] * L[j, k]
                }
                L[i, j] = t / L[j, j]
            }
        }
    }
    return L
}

#| Member `ORTH_CHOL`: X = L^(-T), by back substitution on L^T X = I.
def orth_chol(S: Arr, B: Arr, opts: Arr) -> Arr {
    n = len(S)
    L = orth_cholesky(S)
    X = zeros(n, n)
    # column c of X solves L^T x = e_c; L^T is upper triangular
    for c to n {
        i = n - 1
        while i >= 0 {
            t = 0.0
            if i == c {
                t = 1.0
            }
            k = i + 1
            while k < n {
                t = t - L[k, i] * X[k, c]
                k = k + 1
            }
            X[i, c] = t / L[i, i]
            i = i - 1
        }
    }
    return X
}

#| u^T S v for columns a of U and b of V.
def orth_sdot(S: Arr, U: Arr, a, V: Arr, b) {
    return sum(U[:, a] * (S @ V[:, b]))
}

#| Member `ORTH_GS`: Gram-Schmidt in the S inner product, two passes per vector.
def orth_gs(S: Arr, B: Arr, opts: Arr) -> Arr {
    n = len(S)
    X = zeros(n, n)
    #: orthogonalize twice: one pass leaves rounding
    for c to n {
        X[c, c] = 1.0
        for pass to 2 {
            for p to c {
                X[:, c] = X[:, c] - orth_sdot(S, X, p, X, c) * X[:, p]
            }
        }
        X[:, c] = X[:, c] / sqrt(orth_sdot(S, X, c, X, c))
    }
    return X
}

#| The orthogonalizer registry, production member first.
def orth_methods() {
    return [orth_canon, orth_symm, orth_chol, orth_gs]
}

#| Columns of X that are not all zero: the size m of the orthonormal space.
def orth_rank(X: Arr) {
    m = 0
    for c to len(X) {
        if max(abs(X[:, c])) > 0.0 {
            m = m + 1
        }
    }
    return m
}

#| The orthogonalizer `opts[OPT_ORTH]` names on S: `X[n, n]` with
#| `X^T S X = I` over its first `orth_rank(X)` columns, the dropped columns
#| zero. Each member is called by name, `orth_canon` (the production one)
#| when the option is left at its default.
def orth_of(S: Arr, opts: Arr) -> Arr {
    k = round(opts[OPT_ORTH])
    if k == ORTH_SYMM {
        return orth_symm(S, S, opts)
    }
    if k == ORTH_CHOL {
        return orth_chol(S, S, opts)
    }
    if k == ORTH_GS {
        return orth_gs(S, S, opts)
    }
    return orth_canon(S, S, opts)
}

#| max |X^T S X - I| over the first m columns, formed with compensated sums
#| so the measurement is not limited by its own rounding when X is large.
def orth_defect(S: Arr, X: Arr, m) {
    return max(abs(orth_gram_err(S, X, m)))
}

#| max |X X^T S - I|: X X^T is S^(-1) exactly when X has full rank.
def orth_inverse_defect(S: Arr, X: Arr) {
    E = X @ transpose(X) @ S
    for i to len(E) {
        E[i, i] = E[i, i] - 1.0
    }
    return max(abs(E))
}

#| The worst X^T S X defect over the registry, and how many members did not
#| keep exactly `mwant` columns, for one overlap matrix.
def orth_check_all(S: Arr, mwant) {
    ms = orth_methods()
    o = opt_new()
    worst = 0.0
    nbad = 0
    for k to len(ms) {
        X = apply(ms[k], S, S, o)
        m = orth_rank(X)
        if m != mwant {
            nbad = nbad + 1
        }
        worst = max(worst, orth_defect(S, X, m))
    }
    return worst, nbad
}

test "orth_of runs the member the registry holds at each option value" {
    # a symmetric positive definite S: B B^T plus the identity
    orth_B = eig_random_sym(6, 7)
    orth_S = orth_B @ transpose(orth_B)
    for orth_i to len(orth_S) {
        orth_S[orth_i, orth_i] = orth_S[orth_i, orth_i] + 1.0
    }
    orth_ms = orth_methods()
    for orth_k to len(orth_ms) {
        orth_o = opt_array(ScfOptions { orthogonalizer: orth_k, ..scf_defaults() }, 0, 0, 0.0)
        orth_a = orth_of(orth_S, orth_o)
        orth_b = apply(orth_ms[orth_k], orth_S, orth_S, orth_o)
        assert_near(max(abs(orth_a - orth_b)), 0.0, 0.0)
    }
}

